1
00:00:18,267 --> 00:00:23,110
[Bret] In late May of 1980,
May 23rd to be exact,
The Shining opened,

2
00:00:23,377 --> 00:00:25,615
and I wanted to see it
as soon as possible.

3
00:00:25,616 --> 00:00:27,920
[♪ "Moving in Stereo" playing]

4
00:00:37,873 --> 00:00:41,079
I was obsessed
with Stephen King's
seminal book.

5
00:00:41,080 --> 00:00:44,887
And it remains
one of the key novels
that made me wanna be a writer.

6
00:00:44,888 --> 00:00:47,792
♪♪ Life's the same
I'm moving in stereo ♪

7
00:00:47,793 --> 00:00:51,399
In fact, as soon as I was done
reading The Shining
for a third time,

8
00:00:51,400 --> 00:00:55,843
I began writing my own novel,
which would ultimately become
Less Than Zero.

9
00:00:55,976 --> 00:00:58,447
♪♪ You're shakin' like tremolo ♪

10
00:00:58,548 --> 00:01:02,087
This would be the first time
I drove to Westwood to see
a movie without the gang,

11
00:01:02,088 --> 00:01:05,294
and I felt incredibly adult
as I raced across Mulholland

12
00:01:05,494 --> 00:01:09,671
on that Saturday morning
in my father's
black Mercedes 450 SL,

13
00:01:10,539 --> 00:01:14,913
a two-door tank that was hardly
the sporty vehicle I longed for

14
00:01:14,914 --> 00:01:16,416
as a teenager.

15
00:01:23,096 --> 00:01:27,640
♪♪ It's so easy
To blow up your problems ♪

16
00:01:27,739 --> 00:01:30,176
I remember,
and I don't know why,

17
00:01:30,177 --> 00:01:33,317
that I was wearing
a fashionable new
Ralph Lauren shirt,

18
00:01:33,585 --> 00:01:35,555
brown with a touch
of white-gray stripes

19
00:01:35,655 --> 00:01:38,360
and Calvin Klein jeans
with Gucci loafers.

20
00:01:43,070 --> 00:01:44,573
One ticket, please.

21
00:01:45,909 --> 00:01:47,044
Three dollars.

22
00:02:02,843 --> 00:02:03,945
Thank you.

23
00:02:06,651 --> 00:02:08,555
[♪ upbeat music playing]

24
00:02:10,325 --> 00:02:14,132
I sat in reverent silence
and stared at
the 70-millimeter screen.

25
00:02:15,034 --> 00:02:19,209
Writing this now,
I can't believe I was left to
my own devices for 20 minutes,

26
00:02:19,409 --> 00:02:21,112
just idly sitting there,

27
00:02:21,113 --> 00:02:23,985
thinking about things,
about my friends
Thom and Susan,

28
00:02:23,986 --> 00:02:26,490
waiting without wanting
something to distract me

29
00:02:26,624 --> 00:02:28,761
like smartphones
or social media.

30
00:02:31,534 --> 00:02:36,444
The lights in the theater
slowly darken,
and that's when I saw the boy.

31
00:02:36,877 --> 00:02:39,750
This is the reason why
I've never forgotten
seeing The Shining

32
00:02:39,950 --> 00:02:44,326
on May 24th, 1980,
at the theater in Westwood
at 10:00 a.m.

33
00:02:45,027 --> 00:02:48,535
I was off to the side
near the aisle when I saw him.

34
00:02:48,768 --> 00:02:50,004
[door opens]

35
00:02:51,574 --> 00:02:53,043
[door closes]

36
00:02:54,913 --> 00:02:58,955
A guy so stunningly handsome
I thought at first
he was a movie star

37
00:02:58,956 --> 00:03:01,059
or a model from GQ magazine.

38
00:03:02,729 --> 00:03:03,965
He was tall.

39
00:03:04,098 --> 00:03:06,771
He was undoubtedly
tan everywhere.

40
00:03:08,609 --> 00:03:13,150
This boy aroused
something primal in me
that I'd never felt before.

41
00:03:15,120 --> 00:03:18,126
I needed to be his friend.
I had to own him.

42
00:03:19,830 --> 00:03:23,103
♪♪ Life's the same,
I'm moving in stereo ♪

43
00:03:23,104 --> 00:03:26,578
The Warner Brothers logo
headed toward us
as the opening credits began

44
00:03:26,711 --> 00:03:30,986
over aerial shots
of the family's Volkswagen
heading toward the Overlook.

45
00:03:41,941 --> 00:03:45,582
He had to be older than me.
A UCLA student, maybe.

46
00:03:45,749 --> 00:03:47,953
[♪ suspenseful music playing]

47
00:03:58,675 --> 00:04:05,321
I watched, mesmerized,
as this boy, this god,
disappeared from my sight line.

48
00:04:05,890 --> 00:04:08,460
He noticed me
staring and glanced away,

49
00:04:08,795 --> 00:04:11,099
but not before a lingering gaze
took me in,

50
00:04:11,100 --> 00:04:14,172
and I fantasized that he
was interested in me too.

51
00:04:22,823 --> 00:04:25,193
After the movie was over,
I was underwhelmed.

52
00:04:25,194 --> 00:04:27,432
It was nothing like the book.
I felt cheated.

53
00:04:27,566 --> 00:04:31,540
I walked into the lobby hoping
to find the boy,
but he wasn't anywhere.

54
00:04:33,644 --> 00:04:36,885
I waited outside the theater,
but he wasn't there either.

55
00:04:37,419 --> 00:04:40,925
What I didn't know then is that
I would actually
meet this boy again,

56
00:04:41,025 --> 00:04:43,229
and his name
was Robert Mallory,

57
00:04:43,230 --> 00:04:46,838
and that his arrival
at my high school
and into my life would coincide

58
00:04:47,004 --> 00:04:50,243
with a season of horror
and murder
that would destroy us all.

59
00:04:50,244 --> 00:04:53,316
"Redrum" over and over?
That's gonna haunt me
for the rest of my life.

60
00:04:53,317 --> 00:04:55,555
Like, "redrum"? But...

61
00:05:07,111 --> 00:05:11,620
[Bret] It has occurred to me
when talking about
what happened to us in 1981

62
00:05:11,621 --> 00:05:17,131
that one thing
is evident: Kids today
have it fucking easy.

63
00:05:17,866 --> 00:05:21,038
Sure,
being a teenager is hard now
because you have to deal

64
00:05:21,039 --> 00:05:23,878
with puberty and sexuality
and online bullying,

65
00:05:24,011 --> 00:05:28,486
but our generation had it
tougher because we had to
deal with serial killers.

66
00:05:28,487 --> 00:05:29,823
Shadow.

67
00:05:33,631 --> 00:05:34,700
Shadow.

68
00:05:39,777 --> 00:05:41,346
Shadow.

69
00:05:41,647 --> 00:05:44,486
[Bret] The early '80s
was the golden era
for serial killers.

70
00:05:44,954 --> 00:05:47,290
All the legends
were on hunting sprees.

71
00:05:47,291 --> 00:05:49,697
The Night Stalker,
the Hillside Strangler.

72
00:05:49,930 --> 00:05:51,465
Shadow.

73
00:05:51,466 --> 00:05:53,369
[Bret] As I prepared to
enter senior year...

74
00:05:53,370 --> 00:05:55,273
Shadow.

75
00:05:55,274 --> 00:05:57,378
...I had no idea
that my friends
and I would be hunted too

76
00:05:57,379 --> 00:06:00,752
by perhaps the most
sadistic serial killer
of them all.

77
00:06:02,923 --> 00:06:05,294
[phone ringing]

78
00:06:18,621 --> 00:06:19,422
Hello.

79
00:06:19,656 --> 00:06:21,259
[static buzzing]

80
00:06:24,633 --> 00:06:25,500
Hello.

81
00:06:25,501 --> 00:06:26,904
[bubbling]

82
00:06:28,473 --> 00:06:29,776
Hello.

83
00:06:33,651 --> 00:06:34,953
[hangs up]

84
00:06:53,223 --> 00:06:54,927
[Bret] This is our story.

85
00:06:55,027 --> 00:06:57,131
Some of us
survived high school.

86
00:06:57,132 --> 00:06:58,834
Some of us died.

87
00:06:58,835 --> 00:07:01,139
But none of us
made it out alive.

88
00:07:16,136 --> 00:07:17,371
[pills rattling]

89
00:07:42,956 --> 00:07:45,394
- [man on TV] Darling.
- [screams]

90
00:07:46,095 --> 00:07:47,699
[panting]

91
00:07:48,400 --> 00:07:50,437
[phone ringing]

92
00:07:54,780 --> 00:07:55,615
Hello.

93
00:07:55,848 --> 00:07:57,217
[static buzzing]

94
00:07:58,286 --> 00:08:00,390
[raspy breathing]

95
00:08:03,396 --> 00:08:05,100
- Who is this?
- [line clicks]

96
00:08:33,290 --> 00:08:36,162
- [wind blowing]
- [wind chime tinkles]

97
00:08:36,764 --> 00:08:37,899
[dog barking]

98
00:08:42,041 --> 00:08:43,410
[latch clicks]

99
00:08:45,949 --> 00:08:49,255
[light bulb buzzing]

100
00:08:58,774 --> 00:09:00,043
[dog barking]

101
00:09:32,976 --> 00:09:35,147
[♪ eerie music plays]

102
00:09:57,525 --> 00:09:59,261
[keys jingling]

103
00:10:02,535 --> 00:10:04,105
[screaming]

104
00:10:15,962 --> 00:10:17,097
[thudding]

105
00:10:37,609 --> 00:10:39,074
[♪ "Vienna" ♪]

106
00:10:39,075 --> 00:10:42,648
[Bret] I remember it was
a Sunday afternoon
before Labor Day,

107
00:10:42,649 --> 00:10:46,056
and our senior year
was about to begin
on that Tuesday morning

108
00:10:46,255 --> 00:10:47,960
of September 8th, 1981.

109
00:10:48,327 --> 00:10:50,464
And I remember
that the Westridge Stables

110
00:10:50,565 --> 00:10:53,002
were located on a bluff
above Malibu

111
00:10:53,003 --> 00:10:56,375
where my girlfriend,
Deborah Schaffer, was boarding
her new horse, Spirit.

112
00:10:58,046 --> 00:11:00,985
I was driving solo following
Susan Reynolds and Thom Wright

113
00:11:01,152 --> 00:11:02,621
along Pacific Coast Highway.

114
00:11:02,622 --> 00:11:05,194
♪♪ The feeling has gone
Only you and I ♪

115
00:11:05,327 --> 00:11:08,901
♪♪ It means nothing to me ♪

116
00:11:09,335 --> 00:11:11,740
Everything sped up
when you got your car

117
00:11:11,974 --> 00:11:13,510
- at 16 in Los Angeles.
- ♪♪ Nothing to me ♪

118
00:11:13,611 --> 00:11:16,082
You are now autonomous
in ways you hadn't been.

119
00:11:16,282 --> 00:11:20,023
- You could take care
of yourself.
- ♪♪ Oh, Vienna ♪

120
00:11:20,223 --> 00:11:21,726
You had become an adult.

121
00:11:21,727 --> 00:11:24,298
- [whooping]
- [laughs]

122
00:11:25,935 --> 00:11:28,473
[Bret]
This encouraged our parents
to work longer hours

123
00:11:28,574 --> 00:11:30,544
or travel
with less restrictions,

124
00:11:30,545 --> 00:11:33,515
leaving all of us behind
in beautiful empty houses

125
00:11:33,516 --> 00:11:36,589
in Bel-Air and Beverly Hills
or Benedict Canyon,

126
00:11:36,590 --> 00:11:38,560
and that was okay
with my friends and I.

127
00:11:38,561 --> 00:11:41,298
We were the family.
We raised each other.

128
00:11:41,299 --> 00:11:43,470
[laughing, chattering]

129
00:11:43,571 --> 00:11:46,542
[Bret] We would usually
start our day by meeting
for lunch at Hamburger Hamlet,

130
00:11:46,543 --> 00:11:48,981
where we'd crowd
into the red booths
for patty melts

131
00:11:49,181 --> 00:11:52,253
after buying our tickets
for the next movie
at The Bruin.

132
00:11:52,254 --> 00:11:54,458
Dinner was usually
at the food court

133
00:11:54,459 --> 00:11:57,331
with breaks at the arcade
playing Space Invaders
and PAC-MAN.

134
00:11:59,268 --> 00:12:03,010
Or look for new music
at Tower Records,
leafing through the paperbacks

135
00:12:03,209 --> 00:12:06,382
at any number
of the large bookstores
that dotted the streets.

136
00:12:07,050 --> 00:12:09,120
There were five or six in 1981.

137
00:12:09,121 --> 00:12:10,157
That's the one.

138
00:12:10,323 --> 00:12:12,059
[Bret] There are none now.

139
00:12:12,060 --> 00:12:13,697
[laughing, chattering]

140
00:12:19,475 --> 00:12:23,015
[Bret] I kept behind Thom's car
up the winding road
of the stables

141
00:12:23,016 --> 00:12:26,690
where we parked
next to Deborah's
gleaming brand-new BMW.

142
00:12:35,107 --> 00:12:38,345
We located Debbie
trotting Spirit by his reins.

143
00:12:38,346 --> 00:12:39,849
[whoops] Rock it, Debbie.

144
00:12:39,850 --> 00:12:40,818
[Bret] She was wearing
her riding attire.

145
00:12:41,052 --> 00:12:41,787
There you are.

146
00:12:45,928 --> 00:12:47,196
[Thom whooping]

147
00:12:47,197 --> 00:12:49,168
[whinnies]

148
00:12:49,501 --> 00:12:52,375
[Bret] I was aware of Spirit
being yet another example

149
00:12:52,474 --> 00:12:54,510
of Debbie's carelessness
and privilege.

150
00:12:54,511 --> 00:12:57,951
The cost of maintaining
and housing the animal
was astronomical,

151
00:12:57,952 --> 00:12:59,890
but I don't think
she even cared.

152
00:13:00,992 --> 00:13:03,163
In a way,
Debbie was an experiment.

153
00:13:03,363 --> 00:13:05,834
I wasn't determined
to have a girlfriend
my senior year,

154
00:13:05,835 --> 00:13:07,940
except if it had been
Susan Reynolds,

155
00:13:08,139 --> 00:13:12,514
and yet Debbie
had become somewhat
inexplicably just that.

156
00:13:14,987 --> 00:13:17,224
[♪ pop music playing]

157
00:13:22,100 --> 00:13:26,242
♪♪ I, I, I wanna give you a kiss
I wanna... ♪

158
00:13:29,348 --> 00:13:31,519
[Bret] We were at a party
at Anthony Matthews' house,

159
00:13:31,520 --> 00:13:34,892
and she just started
making out with me
on a chaise lit by the pool.

160
00:13:34,893 --> 00:13:38,767
I was stoned on a Quaalude.
She did a bump of coke.
It was midnight.

161
00:13:38,768 --> 00:13:42,174
- [panting] Put it in.
I'm so wet.
- What?

162
00:13:42,274 --> 00:13:45,815
And I was at a point
where I still tried to be
attracted to girls.

163
00:13:46,082 --> 00:13:49,555
- There's people around.
- I want it. I want you, Bret.

164
00:13:49,556 --> 00:13:51,793
[Bret] I surprised myself
and went with it.

165
00:13:53,363 --> 00:13:55,466
Debbie always broke the rules.

166
00:13:55,467 --> 00:13:58,907
By senior year, her hair was
a shade of platinum
inspired by Blondie.

167
00:13:59,074 --> 00:14:02,814
If you were
in 11th or 12th grade,
makeup was minimally allowed,

168
00:14:02,815 --> 00:14:05,421
but because Debbie was so rich,
she got away with murder

169
00:14:05,554 --> 00:14:07,090
and wore her lipstick hot pink

170
00:14:07,091 --> 00:14:09,461
- or blood red, always defiant.
- [school bell rings]

171
00:14:17,277 --> 00:14:21,451
[laughing, whooping]
Look, Debbie, I'm you.

172
00:14:21,452 --> 00:14:23,590
You guys, cut it out.
You're gonna spook the horses.

173
00:14:23,591 --> 00:14:25,794
- Susan, please,
get off his back.
- [Susan] Okay.

174
00:14:25,795 --> 00:14:28,935
This is
a $1 million horse from Arabia,
and you're gonna spook him

175
00:14:29,168 --> 00:14:31,239
because you can't get off
Thom's back for five minutes.

176
00:14:31,405 --> 00:14:32,674
You just scared all of them...

177
00:14:32,675 --> 00:14:34,377
[Bret]
Thom Wright and Susan Reynolds

178
00:14:34,378 --> 00:14:36,282
had been dating
since they were sophomores

179
00:14:36,382 --> 00:14:38,318
and were now
the most popular people,

180
00:14:38,319 --> 00:14:41,359
not only in our class,
but in the overall
student body.

181
00:14:41,760 --> 00:14:43,397
It was obvious why.

182
00:14:43,496 --> 00:14:45,466
Thom and Susan
were casually beautiful,

183
00:14:45,467 --> 00:14:48,574
all-American,
and they both came
from wealthy families.

184
00:14:49,074 --> 00:14:50,844
For about two years,
they were in love

185
00:14:50,845 --> 00:14:54,285
until that fall of 1981,
when one of them wasn't.

186
00:14:55,420 --> 00:14:57,559
Thom was always the ideal
of teen boy handsome,

187
00:14:57,725 --> 00:15:01,098
and what was so alluring
about him was
that he didn't seem to care.

188
00:15:01,265 --> 00:15:05,007
He had an intriguing blankness
that was attractive
and soothing.

189
00:15:05,173 --> 00:15:07,076
He was the opposite of Susan,

190
00:15:07,077 --> 00:15:10,183
school class president,
effortlessly
a straight-A student.

191
00:15:11,687 --> 00:15:14,959
She kind of knew I was secretly
in love with her,

192
00:15:14,960 --> 00:15:17,098
and I knew
everything about her.

193
00:15:17,599 --> 00:15:20,369
When she got her period,
the problem with her mother,

194
00:15:20,370 --> 00:15:24,111
every imaginary slight
and deprivation
she thought she was enduring...

195
00:15:24,344 --> 00:15:27,885
- I love you.
- ... her crushes on classmates
before Thom.

196
00:15:34,064 --> 00:15:38,273
She was to me
the most beautiful girl ever,

197
00:15:39,308 --> 00:15:43,818
and to this very day,
the person I think I've loved
the most in my entire life.

198
00:15:43,951 --> 00:15:45,921
Have you heard
from your parents?

199
00:15:47,023 --> 00:15:51,099
This morning, still in France,
headed to Venice next week.

200
00:15:51,700 --> 00:15:53,671
They're staying in Europe
indefinitely.

201
00:15:54,204 --> 00:15:56,475
My mom said that
she still thinks
she wants to divorce him.

202
00:15:56,676 --> 00:16:00,349
My dad said that
the trip has been good,
and they weren't coming home

203
00:16:00,350 --> 00:16:03,422
until their marriage was
on solid ground again.

204
00:16:03,423 --> 00:16:05,193
I hope it works out for them.

205
00:16:05,695 --> 00:16:07,766
Sucks having divorced parents.

206
00:16:08,166 --> 00:16:09,737
I miss my dad a lot.

207
00:16:10,336 --> 00:16:12,908
My God, it's my dream,
all that freedom.

208
00:16:12,909 --> 00:16:15,313
Who's cooking your food?
You don't cook.

209
00:16:25,500 --> 00:16:26,769
[Bret] Rosa makes all my meals.

210
00:16:26,770 --> 00:16:28,173
Gracias.

211
00:16:28,807 --> 00:16:31,847
I just write all night
after she goes home
and do whatever I want.

212
00:16:38,561 --> 00:16:40,664
Are you coming
to Debbie's house tonight?

213
00:16:40,665 --> 00:16:42,301
It's the last party
of the summer.

214
00:16:42,535 --> 00:16:45,506
Labor Day always makes me blue.
It's the end of something.

215
00:16:45,507 --> 00:16:48,913
Oh, but this year is the
beginning of our senior year.

216
00:16:48,914 --> 00:16:52,721
We're coming. Although, God,
Debbie's dad makes me
super uncomfortable.

217
00:16:52,722 --> 00:16:55,260
- So goddamn gay.
- Thom!

218
00:16:55,393 --> 00:16:57,565
Sorry, he is.
Last time I was there,

219
00:16:57,765 --> 00:17:01,204
he asked me
if I wanted to take a Jacuzzi
with him in his bathroom,

220
00:17:01,205 --> 00:17:02,875
not the one by the pool.

221
00:17:03,409 --> 00:17:05,414
Pretended I got food poisoning
and left.

222
00:17:05,548 --> 00:17:07,751
Yeah,
I get gay vibes from him too.

223
00:17:07,752 --> 00:17:10,457
I don't think
Debbie's mom knows,
if you could believe it.

224
00:17:10,691 --> 00:17:14,733
She likes the house, the trips,
going to the Oscars every year.

225
00:17:14,866 --> 00:17:17,804
How many does he have again?
Oscars.

226
00:17:17,805 --> 00:17:18,773
Two.

227
00:17:18,774 --> 00:17:20,509
You know that book I'm writing?

228
00:17:20,510 --> 00:17:22,616
I want him to buy it
and let me write the movie.

229
00:17:22,748 --> 00:17:25,553
God, I hate coming here
every weekend just to watch her
ride this dumb horse.

230
00:17:25,554 --> 00:17:30,664
- Why do we do this
to ourselves?
- Because we are nice people.

231
00:17:31,131 --> 00:17:33,469
And this year,
we're gonna rule the school.

232
00:17:33,704 --> 00:17:34,972
[all laughing]

233
00:17:34,973 --> 00:17:36,743
- [Bret] Don't we already?
- [Susan] Help. [squeals]

234
00:17:36,910 --> 00:17:38,513
